page='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Northeast India prehistoric research | AxomRank</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../../../../production/html/book.css"><body><nav class="screen-nav"><a href="../web/index.html">Image library</a><a href="../../../../../production/html/CHAPTER_DESIGN_PLAN.html">Design system</a><a href="../../manuscript/01_Reading_the_Deep_Past.html">Chapter layout proof</a></nav><main class="report"><p class="kicker">BOOK 01 / EDITORIAL RESEARCH / 11 OCTOBER 2026</p><h1>Prehistory across Northeast India</h1><p class="deck">A site-by-site foundation for an Assam exam study book.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Research remains open.</strong><p>Checked passages and original-report acquisition leads have different labels. This inventory covers all eight states but is not an exhaustive census or a completed factual review. Further chapter drafting remains pending.</p></div><h2>What the comparison changes</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Palaeolithic</h3><p>Garo Hills older tool assignments need comparison with celt-manufacturing waste. Padhan’s reassessment questions earlier classifications. This dispute cannot be settled by resemblance alone. See NE09.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Mesolithic</h3><p>Audit Selbalgiri’s claimed sequence and Manipur’s original reports. Small flakes and a Holocene age alone do not establish a microlithic Mesolithic industry. These are research tests, not chapter facts.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Neolithic</h3><p>Compare excavated assemblages with surface collections. Treat tools, pottery, domesticated plants/animals and dates as separate evidence fields. A label in an old report does not prove every part of a farming economy.</p></article></div><h2>A newly added documentary plate</h2><figure class="plate" style="max-width:480px"><div class="image-mat"><img src="Lawnongthroh-F3-0.jpg" alt="Source photograph showing views of facetted and tanged tools from Lawnongthroh, with centimetre scale" style="max-height:360px"></div><figcaption>NE08-F03 / Lawnongthroh, Meghalaya. Source figure 3, Mitri and Neog (2016), p.3. Original scale retained; no generative alteration. Article states CC BY 4.0 on p.17; nested figure credit review remains pending. Tool form alone does not establish age.</figcaption></figure><h2>Site inventory</h2><p>Each row names the present-day state. Older reports using “Assam” may refer to territories in other present-day states. Site-name variants are preserved; coordinate and district verification is pending where not established.</p><input id="filter" placeholder="Filter by site, state, phase or review status" aria-label="Filter site inventory"><table id="sites"><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Site / locality</th><th>State</th><th>Reported classification</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Dating</th><th>Source and locator</th><th>Status / next check</th></tr></thead><tbody>'''+rows+'''</tbody></table><h2>Research programme before Chapter 02 onward</h2><ol><li>Obtain original Palaeolithic and microlithic reports for Garo Hills, Manipur and Tripura; inspect tool drawings and sediment sections.</li><li>Audit lab sample ID, material, layer, depth, uncertainty, method, calibration and association for every proposed date.</li><li>Compare Daojali, Sarutaru and Marakdola with Khasi Hills, Manipur, Arunachal and Nagaland without flattening different periods.</li><li>Require botanical/faunal evidence for farming claims; distinguish observed finds from inferred activity.</li><li>Prepare each site dossier with location variants, excavation history, raw materials, tool technology, pottery, subsistence, burial evidence, chronology disputes and figure permissions.</li><li>Recheck Chapter 01 against the expanded evidence; draft later chapters only when their divisions have locatable support.</li></ol><h2>Evidence and visual plan by chapter</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Research / image commission</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>01 / Reading evidence</td><td>Context and dating diagrams; faithful source plates; study-method conceptual 3D opener.</td></tr><tr><td>02 / Landscapes</td><td>Sourced terrain and river maps; environment records with their own dates.</td></tr><tr><td>03 / Earliest traces</td><td>Claim-versus-reassessment table; original lithic drawings and dated contexts.</td></tr><tr><td>04 / Lifeways</td><td>Cave fauna, bone modifications and botanical evidence; distinguish reconstruction from observation.</td></tr><tr><td>05 / Daojali</td><td>Full excavation and pottery/celt dossier; source scales and sample chronology.</td></tr><tr><td>06 / Regional sites</td><td>State/site comparison map and plates, evidence strength beside each example.</td></tr><tr><td>07 / Crafts</td><td>Reduction sequence, pottery impressions and raw materials, only from checked assemblages.</td></tr><tr><td>08 / Cultivation</td><td>Macroremains, phytoliths and faunal comparisons; no unsupported domestication claims.</td></tr><tr><td>09 / Connections</td><td>Documented similarities, alternative explanations and routes labelled hypothetical where needed.</td></tr><tr><td>10 / Burials</td><td>Dima Hasao jars, burial contexts and dated comparisons; do not transfer Meghalaya dates to Assam jars.</td></tr><tr><td>11 / Early historic</td><td>Layer-specific ceramics and metal finds; distinguish Marakdola from Sarutaru.</td></tr><tr><td>12 / Knowledge limits</td><td>Evidence matrix and revision synthesis after the source audit.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Sources: what was actually consulted</h2>'''+cards+'''<p class="small">Downloaded originals are research references. Redistribution and publication permissions need independent checking. Conceptual art is kept outside this evidence register.</p></main><script>document.querySelector('#filter').addEventListener('input',e=>{for(const r of document.querySelectorAll('#sites tbody tr'))r.hidden=!r.textContent.toLowerCase().includes(e.target.value.toLowerCase())})</script></body></html>'''
